Q: CLIPBRD_E_CANT_OPEN error when setting the Clipboard from .NET A: <p>I ran into this issue as well. Seems to work with this.</p>
<p>I tested the for loop but that one seemed to work erratically. The regular settext did not work at all.</p>
<p>Below I tested a few times, have not tested it Live but seemed to do the trick for the original tests for me at least.</p>
<pre><code>using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Linq;
using System.Runtime.InteropServices;
using System.Runtime.Remoting.Contexts;
using System.Text;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

namespace Microsoft.Hawkeye_VS_Addin {
    public static class Win32Clipboard {
        private const uint CF_UNICODETEXT = 13;

        private const uint GMEM_MOVEABLE = 0x0002;

        [DllImport( &quot;user32.dll&quot;, SetLastError = true )]
        private static extern bool OpenClipboard( IntPtr hWndNewOwner );

        [DllImport( &quot;user32.dll&quot;, SetLastError = true )]
        private static extern bool CloseClipboard();

        [DllImport( &quot;user32.dll&quot;, SetLastError = true )]
        private static extern bool EmptyClipboard();

        [DllImport( &quot;user32.dll&quot;, SetLastError = true )]
        private static extern IntPtr SetClipboardData( uint uFormat, IntPtr hMem );

        [DllImport( &quot;kernel32.dll&quot;, SetLastError = true )]
        private static extern IntPtr GlobalAlloc( uint uFlags, UIntPtr dwBytes );

        [DllImport( &quot;kernel32.dll&quot;, SetLastError = true )]
        private static extern IntPtr GlobalLock( IntPtr hMem );

        [DllImport( &quot;kernel32.dll&quot;, SetLastError = true )]
        private static extern bool GlobalUnlock( IntPtr hMem );

        [DllImport( &quot;kernel32.dll&quot;, SetLastError = true )]
        private static extern IntPtr GlobalFree( IntPtr hMem );

        public static void SetUnicodeText( string text ) {
            if ( text is null ) {
                return;
            }

            // CF_UNICODETEXT requires a NUL-terminated UTF-16 string
            byte[] bytes = Encoding.Unicode.GetBytes( text + &quot;\0&quot; );

            IntPtr hGlobal = IntPtr.Zero;
            IntPtr pGlobal = IntPtr.Zero;

            try {
                hGlobal = GlobalAlloc( GMEM_MOVEABLE, ( UIntPtr )bytes.Length );
                if ( hGlobal == IntPtr.Zero ) {
                    throw new Win32Exception( Marshal.GetLastWin32Error(), &quot;GlobalAlloc failed.&quot; );
                }

                pGlobal = GlobalLock( hGlobal );
                if ( pGlobal == IntPtr.Zero ) {
                    throw new Win32Exception( Marshal.GetLastWin32Error(), &quot;GlobalLock failed.&quot; );
                }

                Marshal.Copy( bytes, 0, pGlobal, bytes.Length );
                GlobalUnlock( hGlobal );
                pGlobal = IntPtr.Zero;

                if ( !OpenClipboard( IntPtr.Zero ) ) {
                    throw new Win32Exception( Marshal.GetLastWin32Error(), &quot;OpenClipboard failed.&quot; );
                }

                try {
                    if ( !EmptyClipboard() ) {
                        return;
                    }

                    // If this succeeds, Windows takes ownership of hGlobal. Do NOT free it.
                    IntPtr result = SetClipboardData( CF_UNICODETEXT, hGlobal );
                    if ( result == IntPtr.Zero )
                        throw new System.ComponentModel.Win32Exception( Marshal.GetLastWin32Error(), &quot;SetClipboardData failed.&quot; );

                    hGlobal = IntPtr.Zero; // ownership transferred
                } finally {
                    CloseClipboard();
                }
            } finally {
                // Only free if we still own it (i.e., SetClipboardData failed)
                if ( pGlobal != IntPtr.Zero )
                    GlobalUnlock( hGlobal );

                if ( hGlobal != IntPtr.Zero )
                    GlobalFree( hGlobal );
            }
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Flutter web deployed on firebase is unable to receive any data though database streams A: <p>Just going to give you a couple of tips to debug this.</p>
<p>1. I'll recommend removing the Map&lt;String,dynamic&gt; type.</p>
<pre><code> body: StreamBuilder(
          stream: db.collection('events').snapshots(),
          builder: (context, snapshot) {
</code></pre>
<p>This is usually the biggest culprit. When the data type returned doesn't map exactly to what is in the collection. Additionally. It's not advisable to stream a collection without a limit to the quantity of items being watched. so you could consider adding a limit.</p>
<p><code>stream:db.collection('events').limit(50).snapshots();</code></p>
<p>Also, consider using a converter to map data from Firebase to Dart types.</p>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code>db = FirebaseFirestore.instance
      .collection('events')
      .withConverter(
        fromFirestore: (snapshot, options) =&gt; EventData.fromJson(snapshot.data()!),
        toFirestore: (data, options) =&gt; data.toJson(),
      );
</code></pre>
<p>This way, your Stream becomes Stream&lt;QuerySnapshot&lt;EventData&gt;&gt;.</p>
<p>2. Check if the firestore read rules is set to allow without authentication. unless you're authenticated. At Firestore database -&gt; Rules.<br />
This is a good rule to get started with</p>
<pre><code>service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    // Allow anyone to read
    match /{document=**} {
      allow read: if true;
    }

    // Allow writes only if the user is authenticated
    match /{document=**} {
      allow write: if request.auth != null;
    }
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>3. Check if your website url is an authorised domain to your firebase, under authentication -&gt; settings -&gt; authorised domains. This should only be useful if you're using authentication.</p>

Q: How to make a parallelogram in React Native A: <p>This is a known bug that's been around for a hot min! lol 2020ish. There's a GitHub issue for it (#27649) that was technically closed with a fix, but clearly it's still broken for a lot of people depending on the RN version or what else you're combining the transform with.</p>
<p>Since you can't really count on the native skew transform working consistently on Android, your best bet is to just draw the shape yourself. If you need a gradient fill anyway, react-native-svg makes this pretty painless:</p>
<pre><code>  import Svg, { Polygon, Defs, LinearGradient, Stop } from 'react-native-svg';

  const Parallelogram = ({ width, height, skew }) =&gt; (
    &lt;Svg width={width + skew} height={height}&gt;
      &lt;Defs&gt;
        &lt;LinearGradient id=&quot;grad&quot; x1=&quot;0&quot; y1=&quot;0&quot; x2=&quot;1&quot; y2=&quot;0&quot;&gt;
          &lt;Stop offset=&quot;0&quot; stopColor=&quot;#ff00ff&quot; /&gt;
          &lt;Stop offset=&quot;1&quot; stopColor=&quot;#0066ff&quot; /&gt;
        &lt;/LinearGradient&gt;
      &lt;/Defs&gt;
      &lt;Polygon
        points={`${skew},0 ${width + skew},0 ${width},${height} 0,${height}`}
        fill=&quot;url(#grad)&quot;
      /&gt;
    &lt;/Svg&gt;
  );
</code></pre>
<p>Or if you're using react-native-skia:</p>
<pre><code>  import { Canvas, Path, LinearGradient, vec } from '@shopify/react-native-skia';

  const Parallelogram = ({ width, height, skew }) =&gt; (
    &lt;Canvas style={{ width: width + skew, height }}&gt;
      &lt;Path
        path={`M ${skew} 0 L ${width + skew} 0 L ${width} ${height} L 0 ${height} Z`}
      &gt;
        &lt;LinearGradient
          start={vec(0, 0)}
          end={vec(width, 0)}
          colors={['#ff00ff', '#0066ff']}
        /&gt;
      &lt;/Path&gt;
    &lt;/Canvas&gt;
  );
</code></pre>
<p>Both work consistently across iOS and Android since you're not relying on the busted transform system at all.</p>

Q: Does a const qualifier inside a struct declaration do anything? A: <ul>
<li><code>const int bar</code> aka <code>int const bar</code> means that <code>bar</code> is a constant <code>int</code>. Once initialized, the field can't be modified directly.</li>
<li><code>int * const bar</code> means that <code>bar</code> is a constant pointer to an <code>int</code>. Once initialized, the field can't be modified directly.</li>
<li><code>const int * bar</code> means that <code>bar</code> is a pointer to a constant <code>int</code>. <code>bar</code> isn't constant and can be modified. It's that to which <code>bar</code> points that's constant.</li>
</ul>
<p>This is easy to check!</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

struct Foo {
    const int bar;  // `bar` is a constant `int`
};

int main( void ) {
   struct Foo foo = { 123 };
   foo.bar = 456;  // error: assignment of read-only member 'bar'
   printf( &quot;%d\n&quot;, foo.bar );
}
</code></pre>
<p><code>const (type) var</code> is just a more readable version of <code>(type) var const</code>. We can use <a href="https://c-faq.com/decl/spiral.anderson.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">spiral rule</a> to explain it what it means.</p>
<p>Same thing:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

struct Foo {
    int const bar;  // `bar` is a constant `int`
};

int main( void ) {
   struct Foo foo = { 123 };
   foo.bar = 456;  // error: assignment of read-only member 'bar'
   printf( &quot;%d\n&quot;, foo.bar );
}
</code></pre>
<p>Changing to a pointer makes no difference:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

struct Foo {
    int * const bar;  // `bar` is a constant pointer to an `int`
};

int main( void ) {
   int i = 123;
   int j = 456;
   struct Foo foo = { &amp;i };
   foo.bar = &amp;j;        // error: assignment of read-only member 'bar'
   *( foo.bar ) = 789;  // ok
   printf( &quot;%d\n&quot;, *( foo.bar ) );
}
</code></pre>
<p>In your second snippet, it's not <code>bar</code> that's constant but <code>*bar</code>.</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

struct Foo {
    const int * bar;  // `bar` is a pointer to a constant `int`
};

int main( void ) {
   int i = 123;
   int j = 456;
   struct Foo foo = { &amp;i };
   foo.bar = &amp;j;        // ok
   *( foo.bar ) = 789;  // error: assignment of read-only location '*foo.bar'
   printf( &quot;%d\n&quot;, *( foo.bar ) );
}
</code></pre>
